  <h1 class="mb-5 mt-0 font-serif text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-[2.8rem]">State Availability</h1>

  <div class="answer-first">
    <span class="af-label">In short</span>
    <p>All research on this site is public everywhere. Commercial offers appear only after you select a state, and are blocked for states where sweepstakes casinos are closed by statute or enforcement.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How the gate works on this site</h2>
  <p>Select your state below. Your choice is stored on your device for 30 days and controls only whether affiliate links are displayed; it never changes which articles you can read.</p>

  <section class="eligibility" aria-label="State selection">
    <h2>Your state</h2>
    <p>Stored locally for 30 days. Nothing is sent to us.</p>
    <div class="js-only">
      <label for="state-picker" class="mb-1.5 block text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500">State</label>
      <select id="state-picker" data-state-picker>
        {% for s in states %}<option value="{{ s.code }}"{% if not s.code %} disabled selected{% endif %}>{{ s.name }}</option>{% endfor %}
      </select>
      <p class="eligibility-note">Current selection: <strong data-state-echo>not selected</strong>.</p>
    </div>
    <noscript><p class="eligibility-note">State selection requires JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </section>

  <h2>Closed states</h2>
  <p>The following states are treated as closed on this site, and commercial offers are blocked for them. This list reflects operator availability policy, not legal advice; state law in this category changes frequently.</p>
  <ul class="tick-list">
    <li class="bad"><b>Closed by statute.</b> California, New York, Connecticut, Indiana, Louisiana, Maine, Montana, Nevada, New Jersey, Oklahoma, Tennessee.</li>
    <li class="bad"><b>Closed by enforcement.</b> Idaho, Michigan, Washington. No sweepstakes-specific law, but regulators apply existing gambling statutes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>California is the most significant recent change: AB 831 was signed on 11 October 2025 and took effect on 1 January 2026.</p>

  <h2>Open states</h2>
  <p>Most other states, including Texas, Florida, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Georgia and North Carolina, are treated as open. Individual operators may still choose not to serve specific states; the operator's own terms govern.</p>

  <h2>Not legal advice</h2>
  <p>This page is general information for readers. Confirm the current rules for your state before you play, and speak to a licensed attorney about your own situation.</p>
